FAIL -- SPEC Problem / lead item 4 (real page).
 1. The save response replaces the card. Probe: a scratch Go test (SCRATCH/probe, never in the
    real tree) posts to the real handleWhatIfSettings with the real renderer. Every POST
    /whatif/settings response (resp_other_older/spouse_older/spouse_spouse) contains
    `<div id="whatif-rate-assumptions-card" hx-swap-oob="true">` wrapping both person rows.
    The path is handlers_rates.go:107 renderRecalc -> handlers.go:195 "whatif-results-with-oob"
    -> whatif.html:286. The rendered card carries 0 data-phase-basis attributes.
 2. Replay (SCRATCH/realpage.cjs). jsdom runs the vendored htmx 2.0.10 plus the real
    whatif-rate-assumptions.js on the real server-rendered card. A fake XHR serves the step-1
    Go-rendered responses, chosen by the posted role and phase. Command:
    `node realpage.cjs out <js> htmx.min.js <delay>`. Results:
      branch, flip back after 100ms: 0 saves and 0 swaps before the flip back; final save phase_age_reference=spouse
      branch, flip back after 450ms: final save phase_age_reference=spouse
      branch, flip back after 700ms: "at flip-back: settings requests so far=1; card swaps=1; same row element? false"
                                     -> "final save carries phase_age_reference=older"
      branch, flip back after 1500ms: final save phase_age_reference=older
      base 6bf3839, every delay: final save phase_age_reference=older
    Slow-path detail (branch): after save #1 (role=other, phase=older), the new row's
    record is {"before":null,"coerced":null}. The flip back then leaves phase=older, and
    request[1] carries person_role[1]=spouse phase_age_reference=older.
    The fix is not inert everywhere: within the debounce window it sends spouse where base
    sends older. On the normal path it matches base.
 3. What is missing: the coercion record has to survive the save-response OOB swap of
    #whatif-rate-assumptions-card, or the design has to change. The current fixture never
    runs a save and swap between the flips, so none of (a)-(d) can see this. The design's
    premise ("per-row state lives on the row element" survives a re-execution) does not hold,
    because the swap replaces the row element itself.
 Harness gaps (disclosed): jsdom needed three shims.
   - A default XPath result type, which htmx omits.
   - A CSS.escape polyfill.
   - Re-setting each onchange attribute after the OOB swap. jsdom does not compile inline
     handlers on nodes adopted from htmx's <template> fragment, and browsers do.
   The page is minimal (#whatif-results plus the card), so the other OOB targets log
   oobErrorNoTarget. GET /whatif/results-full is answered 204. Fake latency is 50 ms; real
   latency only widens the slow path. No other hook preserves the card: the only
   beforeSwap/oobBeforeSwap listeners are base.js (4xx/scroll), income-toast (focus),
   roth-conversion and guardrail_optimizer. There is no hx-preserve in web/templates.

OTHER ITEM-4 FACTS
 - updatePersonRole callers: rate-assumptions.html:63 (server-rendered Role select, onchange) and
   whatif-rate-assumptions.js:160 (addPersonRow's JS-added select, onchange). Both fire on change.
 - togglePhaseReferenceDropdown callers: rate-assumptions.html:14 (use_current_month) and :20 (start_date).
   In whatif-rate-assumptions.js: :27 (updatePersonRole), :68 and :90 (removePersonRow),
   :176 (addPersonRow) and :297 (DOMContentLoaded).
 - Ordering: the inline onchange runs before the form-level `change delay:500ms` request serialises
   the form. The replay shows the restored 'spouse' is in the request when no swap intervenes
   (100 and 450 ms runs).

OBSERVATION (beyond scope, not a FAIL reason): SPEC Fact 3 is only partly borne out. The
POST response for person_role[1]=other with phase_age_reference=spouse renders
phase-age-reference-select=spouse, not older (resp_other_spouse). saveInternal normalises
(settings.go:987), but the rendered response did not reflect it. I did not investigate further.
The client never posts that combination, because the toggle coerces first.
